
CI/CD stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery/Continuous Deployment. It is a DevOps practice that automates the process of building, testing, and deploying software so that code changes can be released quickly and reliably. It is widely used with tools like GitHub, GitLab, Jenkins, Docker, and Kubernetes.

1. Continuous Integration (CI)

Continuous Integration is the practice where developers frequently merge their code changes into a shared repository. Each change triggers an automated build and test process.

Workflow of CI

Developer writes code.

Code is pushed to a repository (e.g., GitHub).

CI tool (like Jenkins or GitHub Actions) detects the change.

The system automatically:

Builds the project

Runs automated tests

Checks code quality

If tests pass → code is merged.

Example CI Pipeline
Developer → Push Code → GitHub Repo
                     ↓
                 CI Tool
                     ↓
      Build → Run Tests → Code Analysis
                     ↓
                 Pass / Fail
Benefits of CI

Detects bugs early

Faster development

Better collaboration

Automated testing

Stable codebase

2. Continuous Delivery (CD)

Continuous Delivery means the application is always ready for deployment. After CI completes, the system prepares the code for release automatically.

However, deployment to production still requires manual approval.

Steps

CI builds and tests the application.

Artifacts are created (Docker image, build package).

Application is deployed automatically to:

staging

testing environments

Manual approval → Production deployment.

Example
Code Push
   ↓
CI Pipeline
   ↓
Build + Test
   ↓
Deploy to Staging
   ↓
Manual Approval
   ↓
Production
3. Continuous Deployment

Continuous Deployment is more advanced than Continuous Delivery.

In this case, every successful code change automatically goes to production without manual approval.

Flow
Developer → Push Code
       ↓
CI Pipeline
       ↓
Build + Test
       ↓
Auto Deploy to Production
Benefits

Very fast release cycles

Less manual work

Immediate feature delivery

Risk

Requires very strong automated testing.

4. CI/CD Pipeline Architecture

A CI/CD pipeline is a sequence of automated steps.

Typical stages:

Source Stage

Code stored in Git repository

Example: GitHub

Build Stage

Compile source code

Create artifacts

Test Stage

Unit tests

Integration tests

Security tests

Package Stage

Create containers using Docker

Deploy Stage

Deploy on servers or containers

Example orchestrator: Kubernetes

